Let's build a PHP site with an auto-login feature using Google OAuth! Here is an example code snippet to get you started:

```php
<?php

session_start();

require 'google-api-php-client/src/Google_Client.php';
require 'google-api-php-client/src/contrib/Google_Oauth2Service.php';

$clientId = "YOUR_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID";

$client = new Google_Client();
$client->setApplicationName("Your Application Name");
$client->setClientId($clientId);
$client->setRedirectUri('http://localhost:8080/callback.html');
$client->addScope(Google_Service_Oauth2::USERINFO_EMAIL);

$google_oauthv2 = new Google_Service_Oauth2($client);

if (isset($_GET['code'])) {
    $token = $google_oauthv2->userinfo_v2_me()->execute();
    $_SESSION["user_email"] = $token["email"];
}

?>

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <title>Login Page</title>
</head>

<body>
    <h1>Google Auto Login Example</h1>
    <!-- Google OAuth button -->
    <a href="<?php echo $client->createAuthUrl(); ?>">Login with your Google account</a>

</body>
</html>
```

To complete this code:

1. Replace "YOUR_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID" with your actual client ID.

2. Set up the Google API project and create an OAuth 2.0 client ID for it.

3. Install the required dependencies by running:
```bash
composer require google/apiclient:^2.0
```

4. Create a `google-api-php-client` directory in your project, add the Google Client library files to it (Google_Client.php and contrib/Google_Oauth2Service.php), and include them in your code using `require`.

5. Replace 'http://localhost:8080/callback.html' with the appropriate redirect URI for your application.

6. Add a callback URL for handling the OAuth response:

```bash
php -S localhost:8080
```

This will create an auto-login feature that redirects users to their Google accounts, then stores their email in the `$_SESSION["user_email"]` variable after successful login.
